WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:03.240
- On a universal geologic timescale,

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:04.690
how might we think about or define

00:00:04.690 --> 00:00:06.800
sustainability of humanity,

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:09.270
as we become a multi-planetary species?

00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:13.324
Sounds like you're dubious of
our multi-planetariness.

00:00:13.324 --> 00:00:14.520
- I am.

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:16.460
So I don't know how to
answer the question,

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:18.460
quite in the context you...

00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:22.300
I do think it is, naive of us

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:25.770
to imagine that in a few generations,

00:00:25.770 --> 00:00:28.140
first of all, that we
could cooperate enough

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:32.500
together to actually transform, say Mars,

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:34.120
which seems the best prospect,

00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:37.920
into anything like a hospitable place.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:40.680
Without so much high-tech intervention

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:42.280
that it's not really
going to be relevant to

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:45.710
the most of the rest of
the population of Earth.

00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:47.960
Because of my understanding of the way

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:50.200
the Earth's system has evolved.

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:53.040
The Earth has not been the same

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:54.680
through all of its history.

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:56.300
I think that's one thing that has changed

00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:59.620
in my time as a Geologist
of 30 years or so.

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:02.313
We see that Earth's story
is kind of a bildungsroman.

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:05.320
It's a developmental story,

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:09.360
and Earth has had many different modes.

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:11.830
Certainly the biggest
biogeochemical change

00:01:11.830 --> 00:01:13.440
was the great oxygenation event

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:15.960
that happened about
1.85 billion years ago,

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:17.940
where we went from an atmosphere that was

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:20.990
probably mostly CO2 and water vapor,

00:01:20.990 --> 00:01:23.010
to one that at least had some free oxygen,

00:01:23.010 --> 00:01:25.500
not 21% like we do now, but some.

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:27.710
Which changed all the
biogeochemical rules,

00:01:27.710 --> 00:01:29.280
and then there was a second jump,

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:31.810
only about 600 million years ago,

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:34.670
to something like near modern values.

00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:37.900
So there are really different
chapters in Earth's story,

00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:40.350
and maybe that could be
sped up a little bit,

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:41.870
on another planet.

00:01:41.870 --> 00:01:44.730
But I mean the timescales here are large,

00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:47.260
and we don't even know
all the players involved.

00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:52.260
We have only a tiny fraction
of the microbes in the world

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:55.950
classified and understood in
terms of their metabolisms,

00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:57.850
and what they're doing
for us behind the scenes,

00:01:57.850 --> 00:02:01.090
so I just find it very naive to think that

00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:05.650
we're gonna be able to go
to another planet and manage

00:02:06.913 --> 00:02:08.573
biogeochemistry on that scale.

00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:11.053
Yeah.

00:02:11.053 --> 00:02:11.917
- Do you think going there and...

00:02:11.917 --> 00:02:12.750
(audience laughs)

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:14.470
Yeah, I agree with you.

00:02:14.470 --> 00:02:17.653
I think though that there's
even value in going and failing.

00:02:18.770 --> 00:02:23.170
That the showing of the
difficulty, in a way...

00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:27.186
If a few humans get there
and they can barely survive,

00:02:27.186 --> 00:02:29.310
and pretty much have to vacate,

00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:30.400
or can only survive,

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:32.770
from massive influxes
of energy from Earth,

00:02:32.770 --> 00:02:34.632
and supplies that--

00:02:34.632 --> 00:02:36.112
- I would argue we can
do that with modeling.

00:02:36.112 --> 00:02:37.000
(laughs)

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:38.390
- Well we can, but I don't think

00:02:38.390 --> 00:02:40.650
it changes the global perception, right?

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:42.650
So it's...

00:02:42.650 --> 00:02:43.940
I think one of the things
you're talking about

00:02:43.940 --> 00:02:46.410
is changing global perception and I think,

00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:48.397
there's all kinds of things
that happen from exploration

00:02:48.397 --> 00:02:50.870
that are beneficial, I think, to humanity

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:53.164
that are intangible in other
ways that going to Mars--

00:02:53.164 --> 00:02:54.230
- I'd like to see the price tag,

00:02:54.230 --> 00:02:56.010
and see whether we could put
that same amount of money

00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:59.184
toward other efforts,

00:02:59.184 --> 00:03:01.790
and I mean there was the Biosphere 2.

00:03:01.790 --> 00:03:04.160
People may remember this
from the early 1990's.

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:07.220
This closed ecosystem or allegedly closed,

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:09.210
in the Arizona Desert,

00:03:09.210 --> 00:03:11.910
where maybe it was the
scale, it was too small,

00:03:11.910 --> 00:03:15.330
but even on that scale,
with everything quantified

00:03:15.330 --> 00:03:16.560
and inventoried very well.

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:18.970
They were not able to
control the oxygen levels,

00:03:18.970 --> 00:03:20.290
and finally had to open the place up,

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:22.020
because the people were
getting really cranky,

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:23.520
and irritable with each other,

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:26.240
because they were getting only 14% oxygen,

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:30.280
and the problem was that
there was too rich soil

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:34.770
in the agricultural
sector of Biosphere 2,

00:03:34.770 --> 00:03:36.300
and even though people
had tried to calculate

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:39.280
exactly how much CO2 that
soil would be respiring,

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:42.070
they were way off, and within a couple of,

00:03:42.070 --> 00:03:43.610
I think it was closed
for about eight months,

00:03:43.610 --> 00:03:46.250
but they had to open it up, because...

00:03:46.250 --> 00:03:47.960
People were just unable to function.

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:50.740
They had altitude sickness
essentially, the whole time.

00:03:50.740 --> 00:03:51.940
- Right

00:03:51.940 --> 00:03:55.030
Yeah, somehow I think these
experiments are helpful,

00:03:55.030 --> 00:03:59.680
for humanity, because I
think a model is a model,

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:01.560
but having, watching humans suffer

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:03.910
is a little bit different,

00:04:03.910 --> 00:04:07.720
and probably will capture
imaginations a little bit better

